  "summary": "Building style with visible wooden frame structure and infill panels.",
  "description": "Characterized by its exposed wooden structural frame with brick, plaster or wattle and daub infill panels. Developed in medieval Germany as an efficient use of timber resources, reflecting the region's forest wealth and the need for durable construction methods.",
  "culturalContext": "German and central European half-timbering (Fachwerk) — the visible oak frame with brick, wattle or plaster infill — built townhouses from the medieval period through the 18th century, its exposed studs, braces and rails becoming regional identity from Franconia's Rhine villages to northern Germany's Deutsche Fachwerkstraße tourist route. Ornament encoded status: ship motifs, inscribed dates and carved sayings on lintels.",
